# open-agents project conventions

This is a Hono + Prisma + pg-boss orchestrator for OpenSandbox-backed agents.
See [AGENTS.md](mdc:AGENTS.md) for the full project guide; this rule
captures the rules every change must follow.

## Module system

- ESM with `module: NodeNext`. Every relative import MUST end in `.js`,
  even though the source is `.ts`:
  ```ts
  import { config } from "./config.js";
  ```
- Use `import type { Foo } from "..."` for type-only imports. ESLint
  enforces this.

## Adding a new agent

There is no longer a code path. Agents are created and configured
through the SPA at `/agents`. The runtime resolves them from the
`Agent` table in Postgres via
[`apps/api/src/agents/service.ts`](mdc:apps/api/src/agents/service.ts)
(`getAgentBySlug`, `getAgentByInboundLocalPart`, etc.). Do NOT add a
`src/agents/<slug>/index.ts` module or an `AGENTS` array — those don't
exist anymore. There is exactly one Mailgun catch-all route per
deployment; the webhook resolves the agent by parsing the recipient.

## Tools (unified catalog)

Every agent capability — OpenSandbox sandbox tools (`bash`, `read`,
`web_search`, …) and our own platform handlers — is a row in the `Tool`
table, discriminated by `runtime` (`managed` | `platform`). One binding
table (`AgentToolBinding`) references both. OpenSandbox translates these rows
to Pi tools at run time; the `runtime` discriminator is the only thing
application code should care about.

External MCP servers live in `McpServer` and attach via `AgentMcpBinding`. They
are per-agent endpoints, not catalog rows.

### Defining a platform tool

Platform tools live under `apps/api/src/mcp/platform/<key>.ts` as
handler bundles. Use `defineTool` from
[`apps/api/src/mcp/types.ts`](mdc:apps/api/src/mcp/types.ts); the Zod
schema is the single source of truth and the handler receives typed,
parsed input plus a `ctx` with `agentId`, `bindingId`, and decrypted
per-binding `secrets`.

```ts
defineTool({
  name: "…",
  description: "…",
  input: z.object({ … }),
  handler: async (input, ctx) => { … },
});
```

Append the handler to `PLATFORM_HANDLERS` in
[`apps/api/src/mcp/platform/index.ts`](mdc:apps/api/src/mcp/platform/index.ts).
A boot-time `seedToolCatalog()` call upserts a `Tool` row with
`runtime = "platform"` so the UI tool picker stays in sync, alongside
the managed-runtime rows for the toolset. Don't insert `Tool` rows by
hand.

## Layering rules

- `routes/`  → calls into `services/` and enqueues jobs. No Prisma writes
  inline; extract to `services/*`.
- `jobs/`    → orchestration shells. Heavy lifting goes in `services/`.
- `services/` → reusable domain logic. May import `db.ts`, `mailgun/*`,
  `agents/*`, `mcp/*`, `secrets/*`. Must NOT import from
  `routes/` or `jobs/<workerName>.ts`.
- `agents/`, `mcp/`, `mailgun/`, `secrets/`, `auth/` →
  leaf-ish modules. They may import `db.ts`, `log.ts`, `config.ts`, and
  each other (where the dependency is acyclic).
- Cross-cutting helpers shared by `routes/` AND `jobs/` go in `services/`.

## Database (Prisma 7)

The schema lives at `packages/db/prisma/schema.prisma`. Prisma 7 conventions:

- The `prisma-client` generator emits **TypeScript source** to
  `packages/db/generated/prisma/`, which `tsc` then compiles to
  `packages/db/dist/prisma/`. Consumers always import from `@open-agents/db`
  (the workspace package), never from the generated path directly.
- The `datasource` block in `schema.prisma` carries only the provider.
  Connection URL + migration paths live in
  [`packages/db/prisma.config.ts`](mdc:packages/db/prisma.config.ts).
- `prisma.config.ts` loads the env file (root `.env` or
  `apps/api/.env`) explicitly via `dotenv` — Prisma 7 no longer auto-loads.
- The Prisma client is constructed with the
  [`@prisma/adapter-pg`](https://www.prisma.io/docs/orm/core-concepts/supported-databases/database-drivers)
  driver adapter (Prisma 7 has no Rust query engine for SQL; an adapter
  is required). The singleton lives at `apps/api/src/db.ts`.

After editing `schema.prisma`:

```bash
pnpm db:migrate -- --name <slug>   # interactive, requires DATABASE_URL
pnpm db:generate                    # regenerate the client
```

`postinstall` runs both for you on a clean checkout, so any teammate that
just pulls the repo and `pnpm install`s gets a working client.

## Logging

Use `log` from [`apps/api/src/log.ts`](mdc:apps/api/src/log.ts). Pass
structured context as a meta object, never string-concatenate. Don't
`console.log` — ESLint will flag it.

## Required after every edit

```bash
pnpm check
```

Runs Turborepo's `typecheck` + `lint` across every workspace package and
then `prettier --check` over the repo. All three must be green before
committing.

If your change requires updating the documentation, please do so after
you changed the last file and all checks run green. This is needed so
the documentation is not allowed to drift. Touch every relevant doc:
`docs/*.md`, READMEs, in-code comments, and `AGENTS.md`.

## Common gotchas

- New attachments mount into existing OpenSandbox sandboxes via
  `mountSessionResources`; don't rotate sessions just to add files.
- Service credentials (model providers, Mailgun) live AES-GCM encrypted in
  the `Secret` table and are read via
  [`apps/api/src/secrets/service.ts`](mdc:apps/api/src/secrets/service.ts).
  Don't add new env vars for service credentials — they belong in the
  DB so admins can rotate them through the UI.
